WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.822
Code review begins with high-signal
construction patterns.

2
00:00:03.902 --> 00:00:06.649
Search for SQL keywords inside template
literals,

3
00:00:06.729 --> 00:00:10.179
concatenation near query calls,
interpolation in raw methods,

4
00:00:10.259 --> 00:00:14.603
dynamically assembled WHERE or ORDER BY
clauses, literal-expression helpers,

5
00:00:14.683 --> 00:00:17.685
and generic functions that accept complete
fragments.

6
00:00:17.765 --> 00:00:21.790
These patterns identify places where
values may cross into SQL structure.

7
00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:23.786
Review wrappers as well as callers;

8
00:00:23.866 --> 00:00:28.019
a helper named safeQuery provides no
assurance if it concatenates arguments

9
00:00:28.099 --> 00:00:28.802
internally.

10
00:00:28.882 --> 00:00:31.181
Trace every query component to its source.

11
00:00:31.261 --> 00:00:35.797
Mark fixed SQL syntax, bound values, and
dynamic structural fragments separately.

12
00:00:35.877 --> 00:00:39.582
Confirm that external values reach the
driver's parameter channel.

13
00:00:39.662 --> 00:00:43.112
When a request affects a column, operator,
direction, or table,

14
00:00:43.192 --> 00:00:46.834
locate the closed server-side mapping that
produces the fragment.

15
00:00:46.914 --> 00:00:51.449
Verify ORM raw methods and tagged-template
semantics from library documentation.

16
00:00:51.529 --> 00:00:54.532
Also examine tenant scope, authorization
conditions,

17
00:00:54.612 --> 00:00:56.528
and database-account privileges.

18
00:00:56.608 --> 00:01:00.633
Integration tests should exercise ordinary
and unexpected input as data.

19
00:01:00.713 --> 00:01:03.971
Include punctuation, Unicode,
long-but-allowed strings,

20
00:01:04.051 --> 00:01:08.267
empty values, optional-filter
combinations, dynamic sort choices, arrays,

21
00:01:08.347 --> 00:01:10.200
and rejected structural choices.

22
00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:14.241
Assert results and protected database
state, not merely response codes.

23
00:01:14.321 --> 00:01:17.707
Add regression tests around previously
unsafe construction.

24
00:01:17.787 --> 00:01:22.195
A catalog of offensive payloads is less
reliable than proving that request values

25
00:01:22.275 --> 00:01:24.383
never enter generated statement text.

26
00:01:24.463 --> 00:01:27.913
Driver mocks can verify
statement-and-parameter separation,

27
00:01:27.993 --> 00:01:32.145
while selected real-database tests catch
dialect and binding differences.

28
00:01:32.225 --> 00:01:35.291
Static analysis and lint rules can flag
raw-query calls,

29
00:01:35.371 --> 00:01:38.566
SQL-bearing template literals, or banned
unsafe helpers.

30
00:01:38.646 --> 00:01:42.670
Route exceptions through explicit review
rather than quiet suppression.

31
00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:47.030
Keep raw SQL in a small searchable
data-access surface and require tests for its

32
00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:47.941
dynamic paths.

33
00:01:48.021 --> 00:01:51.407
Automated checks will not understand every
library contract,

34
00:01:51.487 --> 00:01:53.212
so pair them with design review.

35
00:01:53.292 --> 00:01:57.636
The strongest review question remains
consistent: does this API bind the value,

36
00:01:57.716 --> 00:01:59.632
or insert text into the SQL program?

37
00:01:59.712 --> 00:02:04.184
Answering from implementation and
documentation is more dependable than judging

38
00:02:04.264 --> 00:02:06.500
whether a sample input appears harmless.
